From e1b666f9ba9afd02ecf6b018a760cbf83e43bbe2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 06:40:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Two-tone test signal on TUNE, a movable TRANSMIT window,
 and keep up with fast receivers through a full-duplex over (#525)

A 2T chip beside TUNE transmits 700 Hz and 1900 Hz together at the tune
level until clicked again, like the Tone button other SDR programs have: the
signal amplifier linearity is judged with, and the one PureSignal learns
from, since a steady carrier has a single amplitude. TxState gains two_tone
and Command gains SetTuneTwoTone; wire protocol v188. Keying and level are
TUNE's own, so every guard on TUNE applies.

On the compact strip the TX chip's drop-down hung over the S-meter, which is
what an operator watches while setting drive and tune. It now opens a
TRANSMIT window instead: the same controls, draggable clear of the meter,
and open through an over until closed.

During a full-duplex over the engine read one 16384-sample block per 10 ms
tick, a 1.64 Msps ceiling: a 2.083 Msps PlutoSDR dropped a fifth of its
receive, which is also PureSignal's feedback. It now takes up to four blocks
a tick when that many are already waiting, still never waiting for more, and
each goes to the panadapter as well as the audio, as #652 made it.
